        "knowledge_point": "Determining Whether Three Vectors are Coplanar",
        "problem": "Find $t$ given that the vectors $p=⟨t,t,1⟩,$ $q=⟨3,2,6⟩,$ and $r=⟨3,4,0⟩$ are coplanar.",
        "worked_solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =|\\begin{aligned}t & t & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|-t\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t(2\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 6)-t(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 6)+(3\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-24t+18t+6 \\\\  & =-6t+6 \\\\  & =6(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 6(1-t) & =0 \\\\ 1-t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=1.$",
