        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function",
        "problem": "If $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+9x,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$",
        "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=3{x}^{3}+9x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3{y}^{3}+9y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{3}+9y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =9{y}^{2}+9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
