        "knowledge_point": "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor",
        "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({4}^{x}+{x}^{2})(2x-3)\\ge 0.\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "Since ${4}^{x}+{x}^{2}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({4}^{x}+{x}^{2})(2x-3)}{{4}^{x}+{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\frac{0}{{4}^{x}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
