        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position",
        "problem": "The displacement (in centimeters) of an ant moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+3t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds.\nFind the ant's acceleration at $t=10$ seconds.",
        "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+3t) \\\\  & =2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=10,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\na(10)=2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=10$ seconds is $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$",
